======================================================================================================================
ITEM 94 -- distances from the cosmological constant: a ladder-free H_0, cross-checked against TRGB
======================================================================================================================
  SPARC after the standard cuts (Q<=2, inc>=30 deg, >=6 points): 147 galaxies -- Hubble flow 79, TRGB 37, Cepheid 3, UMa cluster 26, SNe 2
  fiducial Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.5 (McGaugh & Schombert 2014 stellar populations), scanned 0.3-0.7; bulge 0.7
  NOT circular, and this must be said: item 76 of this hunt DERIVES Upsilon from a_0 by putting the deep tail on the RAR at SPARC's catalogue distances.  Using that Upsilon here would force D_RAR = D_cat by construction.  The Upsilon used below is the external stellar-population value 0.5, fixed before any distance is computed.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A -- the estimator is distance-free.  Proof, not assertion.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     NGC2403    catalogue D =   3.16 Mpc -> D_RAR =  3.739 Mpc;  with the catalogue distance moved to x1.20 and x0.70 (and the whole mass model rescaled with it):  3.739,  3.739 Mpc
     NGC5055    catalogue D =   9.90 Mpc -> D_RAR =  8.211 Mpc;  with the catalogue distance moved to x1.20 and x0.70 (and the whole mass model rescaled with it):  8.211,  8.211 Mpc
     DDO154     catalogue D =   4.04 Mpc -> D_RAR =  3.503 Mpc;  with the catalogue distance moved to x1.20 and x0.70 (and the whole mass model rescaled with it):  3.503,  3.503 Mpc
     NGC7814    catalogue D =  14.40 Mpc -> D_RAR = 19.822 Mpc;  with the catalogue distance moved to x1.20 and x0.70 (and the whole mass model rescaled with it): 19.822, 19.822 Mpc
     UGC00128   catalogue D =  64.50 Mpc -> D_RAR = 70.733 Mpc;  with the catalogue distance moved to x1.20 and x0.70 (and the whole mass model rescaled with it): 70.733, 70.733 Mpc
  [PASS] 94a the distance estimator is EXACTLY ladder-free: moving a galaxy's assumed catalogue distance by +20% or -30%, with r, V_gas, V_disk and V_bul all rescaled consistently, leaves the recovered absolute distance unchanged to machine precision.  g_bar is a distance invariant and g_obs carries the whole 1/D dependence   (worst fractional change 2.22e-16 over five galaxies)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART B -- the item's route (BTFR as an absolute standard), and why it is wrong by 30%
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  canonical  D_BTFR/D_TRGB: N = 27, median +0.1132 dex (+29.8%), mean +0.1143, scatter 0.100 dex
  alt        D_BTFR/D_TRGB: N = 27, median +0.0723 dex (+18.1%), mean +0.0734, scatter 0.100 dex
  the diagnosis, on the 27 TRGB galaxies, at their outermost rotation-curve point:
     median y = g_bar/a_0 there is 0.044 -- low, but NOT zero, and the deep-MOND asymptote assumes zero
     finite-y term       log10[nu(y) sqrt(y)]      = +0.0447 dex
     disc-geometry term  0.5 log10(M_enc/M_tot)    = +0.0361 dex   (V_bar^2 r/G exceeds the disc's mass by 18% -- a thin disc, not a sphere)
     V_flat-vs-outermost 2 log10(V_flat/V_out)     = -0.0015 dex
     sum +0.0793 dex against the measured log10(D_BTFR/D_RAR) = +0.0789 dex; the identity closes per galaxy to 0.0000 dex
     consequence, computed and not asserted: run through the H_0 machinery of Part D, the BTFR route returns H_0 = 53.7 km/s/Mpc from the same 29 flow galaxies -- 14 below Planck. The +0.113 dex distance error is the whole of it.
  [PASS] 94b THE ITEM'S ROUTE AS POSED IS WRONG, and the cause is decomposed exactly.  Treating the BTFR as an absolute standard uses the deep-MOND asymptote V^4 = G M_b a_0, which assumes y = g_bar/a_0 = 0 and a spherical mass.  Neither holds: the outermost point of a real rotation curve sits at y ~ 0.04, worth +0.045 dex, and a thin disc's V^2 r/G exceeds its enclosed mass by ~18%, worth another +0.036 dex.  The two terms plus the small V_flat-versus-outermost-point term reproduce the BTFR route's excess exactly, galaxy by galaxy.  The item's 'BTFR distances agree with TRGB to <= 5%' bar cannot be met by the BTFR form; only the full kernel can meet it   (the decomposition closes to 0.0000 dex per galaxy; D_BTFR/D_TRGB = +0.1132 dex (+30%), of which +0.0789 dex is the BTFR-versus-kernel gap and the remainder is where the kernel route itself sits relative to TRGB)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART C -- the kernel route (D_RAR) against the TRGB distances
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  canonical  log10(D_RAR/D_TRGB): N = 37   median -0.0113 +- 0.0289 (-2.6%)   mean -0.0696 +- 0.0357 (-14.8%)   per-galaxy scatter 0.219 dex
                excluding the four lowest-mass dwarfs (log M_b < 8.5, whose curves barely resolve): N = 28, median -0.0085, mean -0.0150
  alt        log10(D_RAR/D_TRGB): N = 37   median -0.0458 +- 0.0286 (-10.0%)   mean -0.1059 +- 0.0356 (-21.6%)   per-galaxy scatter 0.218 dex
                excluding the four lowest-mass dwarfs (log M_b < 8.5, whose curves barely resolve): N = 28, median -0.0409, mean -0.0511
  the mean and the median differ by 0.06 dex because the tail is one-sided: the four worst galaxies are CamB (-0.82 dex, log M_b = 7.7), UGC07577 (-0.62 dex, log M_b = 7.9), NGC4068 (-0.38 dex, log M_b = 8.5), UGC08837 (-0.36 dex, log M_b = 8.8) -- all near-unresolved dwarfs.  Both statistics are reported; neither is chosen for looking better.
  [PASS] 94c the kernel route's TRGB agreement MEETS the item's <= 5% bar on the canonical footing and MISSES it on the alt footing -- in the median.  It misses on both footings if the mean is used instead, because four near-unresolved dwarfs pull it.  A 5%-in-the-median agreement across 37 galaxies whose per-galaxy scatter is 0.22 dex is a statement about the sample mean, not about any one distance   (canonical median -2.6% (mean -14.8%), alt median -10.0% (mean -21.6%); per-galaxy scatter 0.219 dex = 66%)

  the Upsilon-free version: on gas-dominated galaxies the stellar mass-to-light ratio drops out of g_bar, so what is left measures a_0 alone against the TRGB distance scale.
     canonical  gas-dominated (f_gas > 0.5) TRGB galaxies: N = 12, log10(D_RAR/D_TRGB) = -0.0612 +- 0.0555
     alt        gas-dominated (f_gas > 0.5) TRGB galaxies: N = 12, log10(D_RAR/D_TRGB) = -0.0985 +- 0.0548
     solving numerically for the a_0 that zeroes that offset (no deep-MOND scaling assumed): a_0 = 6.841e-11 +- 2.0e-11 m/s^2, -0.136 dex from canonical and -0.218 dex from alt
  BUG FOUND AND FIXED IN THE MAKING: the first version of this block used the analytic deep-MOND scaling D ~ a_0^(-1/2) and entered the exponent with the wrong sign, which put the implied a_0 ABOVE canonical instead of below.  The check failed, the algebra was re-derived, and the solve is now numerical.
  [PASS] 94d AGAINST INTEREST -- the one genuinely Upsilon-free measurement in this item pulls a_0 BELOW the canonical footing.  Twelve gas-dominated galaxies with TRGB distances want a_0 near 7e-11 m/s^2, about 0.14 dex under canonical and 0.22 dex under alt.  Neither footing is excluded by it -- canonical sits ~1 sigma high, alt ~2 sigma high -- and twelve galaxies is a pull, not a measurement   (a_0 = 6.84e-11 +- 2.0e-11 (N = 12); canonical is 1.1 sigma above it, alt 1.8 sigma above it)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART C2 -- the estimator's OWN floor, measured on a sample that is all at the same catalogue distance
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SPARC gives every one of its Ursa Major cluster members (f_D = 4) the IDENTICAL catalogue distance, 18.0 +- 2.5 Mpc -- verified below, not assumed.  The spread of D_RAR across those galaxies is therefore the estimator's own per-galaxy precision plus the cluster's real line-of-sight depth, with no distance-catalogue error in it at all.  The TRGB comparison cannot give that number, because there the catalogue distances carry their own errors and a real 1-17 Mpc spread.
  [PASS] 94g DATA CONTROL (can fail): every Ursa Major galaxy really does carry one and the same catalogue distance, so the scatter measured below contains no catalogue-distance error by construction   (25 UMa galaxies, distinct catalogue distances [18.0])
     ladder-free distance to the Ursa Major cluster: D_RAR = 17.7 +- 0.9 Mpc (stat) from 25 members on the canonical footing, 16.6 Mpc on the alt footing, against SPARC's assumed 18.0 and the published cluster distance 17-19 Mpc (Tully+2000; Sorce+2013).
     and immediately against interest: that ZERO POINT is not independent of Part C.  It is the same RAR normalisation -- a_0 fixed by Lambda with Upsilon = 0.5 -- that put the TRGB median at -2.6%, so it is one measurement quoted twice, not two agreements.  The new content of this section is the SCATTER, which the TRGB sample cannot isolate.
     per-galaxy scatter of log10 D_RAR: Ursa Major (one true distance) 0.100 dex, TRGB sample 0.222 dex, TRGB restricted to the UMa mass range (log M_b 9.2-10.9, N = 14) 0.118 dex.
     the TRGB distances' own errors are a median 5.0% = 0.022 dex, i.e. 1% of the TRGB variance.
  [PASS] 94h the per-galaxy floor belongs to the ESTIMATOR, not to the distance catalogue, and it splits the sample in two.  On galaxies at one and the same catalogue distance the recovered ladder-free distances scatter by 0.10 dex (26%), and a MASS-MATCHED TRGB subsample reproduces that -- so the full TRGB sample's 0.22 dex is carried by the near-unresolved dwarfs, not by the distances, whose own errors are a tenth of it in quadrature.  Both readings are recorded: a well-resolved spiral's ladder-free distance is good to about a quarter, a dwarf's is not good at all, and item 94's '<= 5%' bar is reachable only in the mean of a sample   (UMa (one true distance) 0.100 dex = 26% vs mass-matched TRGB 0.118 dex (ratio 0.84) vs full TRGB 0.222 dex; TRGB catalogue-distance error 0.022 dex = 1% of the variance.  The UMa figure is an UPPER bound on the estimator's precision, because the cluster's real line-of-sight depth is folded into it: a uniform +-2 Mpc half-depth about 18 Mpc is an rms of 0.028 dex, which would leave 0.096 dex for the estimator -- so this one runs in the framework's favour and is stated that way)
  
  a distance-dependent systematic would show as a trend of the residual with distance.  This is NOT an independent confirmation that D_RAR tracks D_TRGB and must not be quoted as one: D_RAR is built from a table written at D_TRGB and is exactly invariant to it (Part A), so the regression slope of log D_RAR on log D_TRGB is 1 + this trend by construction.  What the trend does test is beam smearing, resolution and Malmquist selection.
  WRITTEN AGAINST INTEREST AND THEN FAILED: the first version of the check below asserted that the trend IS detected, in the direction a resolution systematic predicts.  It is not -- +0.124 +- 0.127 is 1.0 sigma.  That assertion failed and is recorded here instead of being retuned; the check is restated as the weaker thing the 37 galaxies actually support.
  [PASS] 94i AGAINST INTEREST -- no distance-dependent systematic is DETECTED, but the sample is nowhere near able to exclude one at the size that matters.  The allowed trend, propagated across the sample's own distance range, is an order of magnitude larger than the TRGB agreement being quoted from it.  The -2.6% median is therefore not shown to be free of a resolution or Malmquist systematic; it is only not shown to have one   (d log10(D_RAR/D_TRGB) / d log10 D_TRGB = +0.124 +- 0.127 (1.0 sigma from zero, not a detection); over the sample's 1.25 dex in distance the 1-sigma allowed drift is +-0.158 dex, against the 0.011 dex median agreement it would have to protect -- a factor 14 short)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART D -- H_0 from the Hubble-flow subsample.  V_flow = 73 * D_cat by SPARC's own definition, so the 73 cancels:
            H_0 = V_flow / D_RAR = 73 * D_cat / D_RAR = 73 * 10^(-offset).
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  footing canonical
    V_flow >     0 km/s: N =  76   H_0 =  69.38 +- 2.64 (log-mean)    67.52 (median)   weighted fit  68.07
    V_flow >  1000 km/s: N =  47   H_0 =  71.32 +- 3.37 (log-mean)    69.89 (median)   weighted fit  69.11
    V_flow >  2000 km/s: N =  33   H_0 =  68.69 +- 3.36 (log-mean)    70.33 (median)   weighted fit  69.13
    V_flow >  3000 km/s: N =  26   H_0 =  70.37 +- 3.78 (log-mean)    71.99 (median)   weighted fit  70.63
    all 76 flow galaxies, weighted fit with sigma_pec = 250 km/s:  68.07
  footing alt
    V_flow >     0 km/s: N =  76   H_0 =  74.94 +- 2.87 (log-mean)    73.49 (median)   weighted fit  73.16
    V_flow >  1000 km/s: N =  47   H_0 =  76.90 +- 3.65 (log-mean)    75.79 (median)   weighted fit  74.24
    V_flow >  2000 km/s: N =  33   H_0 =  73.87 +- 3.61 (log-mean)    75.79 (median)   weighted fit  74.18
    V_flow >  3000 km/s: N =  26   H_0 =  75.61 +- 4.06 (log-mean)    77.39 (median)   weighted fit  75.75
    all 76 flow galaxies, weighted fit with sigma_pec = 250 km/s:  73.16
  the ladder alternative sits beside it: Planck 67.4 +- 0.5, SH0ES 73.0 +- 1.0.
  [PASS] 94e a ladder-free H_0 IS computable, to a statistical precision of about +-3.4 km/s/Mpc from 33 flow galaxies -- and the two a_0 footings sit on opposite sides of the Hubble tension.  The canonical footing lands on Planck, the alt footing lands on SH0ES, and the gap between the footings is as large as the gap between the two camps.  This route therefore cannot arbitrate the tension; it can only be told which side it is on   (canonical H_0 = 68.7 +- 3.4 (Planck 67.4, 0.4 sigma); alt H_0 = 73.9 +- 3.6 (SH0ES 73.0, 0.2 sigma); footing gap 5.2 against the tension's 5.6 km/s/Mpc -- the same size, so the footing alone decides which camp this route agrees with)

