PART E -- the error budget, scanned rather than asserted
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.3: H_0 =  55.88   TRGB median offset +0.0653 dex (+16.2%)
    Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.4: H_0 =  62.50   TRGB median offset +0.0114 dex (+2.7%)
    Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.5: H_0 =  68.69   TRGB median offset -0.0113 dex (-2.6%)
    Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.6: H_0 =  74.37   TRGB median offset -0.0596 dex (-12.8%)
    Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.7: H_0 =  80.14   TRGB median offset -0.0841 dex (-17.6%)
    g_bar < 0.3 a_0 (deep only) : H_0 =  67.59  (N = 30)
    g_bar < a_0 (fiducial)      : H_0 =  68.69  (N = 33)
    every point                 : H_0 =  68.75  (N = 32)
    the zero point is NOT the same in every subsample: median offset +0.0339 (Hubble flow), -0.0113 (TRGB), -0.0069 (Ursa Major cluster) -- a 11% spread between the two that matter, at matched baryonic mass as well as overall
  [PASS] 94f the systematic floor is several times the statistical error, and the stellar mass-to-light ratio and the subsample zero point share it about equally.  H_0 scales as sqrt(Upsilon), so the stellar-population uncertainty alone moves it by more than the whole Hubble tension, the footing moves it by about the tension, and the flow-versus-TRGB zero point moves it by more again.  The item's '+-3 km/s/Mpc' bar is missed on systematics, not on statistics, and the exact factor is printed rather than rounded   (Upsilon 0.3-0.7 spans H_0 = 55.9-80.1 (half-range 12.1), and Upsilon = 0.5 +- 0.1 alone is +-5.9; footing spans 68.7-73.9 (+5.2); subsample zero-point difference 11% = 7.4; quadrature floor 9.5 against a statistical error 3.4 (2.8x) and against the item's bar of 3.0 (3.2x))
  turned round, which is the more useful statement: at a FIXED H_0 the same estimator measures the product a_0 * Upsilon, because D goes as (a_0 Upsilon_eff)^(-1/2) wherever the stars dominate:
     assuming H_0 = Planck 67.4 : the flow sample requires a_0 * (Upsilon/0.5) = 9.011e-11 m/s^2 (-0.016 dex from canonical, -0.098 dex from alt)
     assuming H_0 = SH0ES 73.0  : the flow sample requires a_0 * (Upsilon/0.5) = 1.057e-10 m/s^2 (+0.053 dex from canonical, -0.029 dex from alt)

