MUTATION CONTROL
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] 94-M1 setting nu = 1 (Newtonian gravity, no dark matter, the same photometry) must WRECK the estimator: the recovered distances become the mass discrepancy in disguise and H_0 collapses.  If it did not, the kernel would not be doing the work   (Newtonian H_0 = 22.7 km/s/Mpc (against 68.7 with the kernel); Newtonian TRGB offset +0.493 dex = +211% too long)
  [PASS] 94-M4 the Ursa Major precision test must also break under nu = 1.  Twenty-five galaxies at ONE true distance, run through Newtonian gravity with the same photometry, must scatter far more than they do through the kernel -- their recovered distances then differ by their mass discrepancies, which is what the 0.10 dex would be measuring if the kernel were doing nothing   (Newtonian UMa scatter 0.204 dex vs 0.100 dex with the kernel (factor 2.0))
  [PASS] 94-M2 shuffling which TRGB distance belongs to which galaxy must destroy the agreement.  The real median offset must sit far in the low tail of the shuffled distribution   (real |median offset| 0.0113 dex sits at the 13th percentile of 2000 shuffles (median shuffled |offset| 0.055 dex))
  [PASS] 94-M3 a_0 mutated by a factor 10 must move H_0 by about sqrt(10) = 3.16, since D goes as a_0^(-1/2) in the deep regime -- the estimator's sensitivity to a_0 is the whole point, and it is checked here rather than assumed   (H_0(10 a_0)/H_0(a_0) = 2.54 against the predicted 3.16 (the shortfall is the finite-y correction: not every point is deep))

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- item 94
======================================================================================================================
  PARTIAL PASS, and the item's own route is the part that fails.
  (1) The estimator is exactly ladder-free and that is proven, not argued: g_bar is a distance invariant and g_obs
      carries the whole 1/D, so moving a galaxy's assumed distance by 20-30% leaves the recovered distance
      unchanged to machine precision.  That is a real property of the framework, not of this script.
  (2) The item's BTFR-as-absolute-standard route is wrong by +0.113 dex (+30% in distance, H_0 = 54), and the cause
      is pinned exactly: the outermost point of a rotation curve sits at y = g_bar/a_0 = 0.044, not at the
      deep-MOND asymptote's y = 0, and a thin disc's V^2 r/G is not its enclosed mass.  The two terms close the gap
      galaxy by galaxy.  The BTFR form must not be quoted as an absolute distance indicator; the full kernel must be.
  (3) With the full kernel the TRGB agreement is -2.6% in the median on the canonical footing -- inside the
      item's 5% bar -- and -10.0% on the alt footing.  The per-galaxy scatter is 66%, so this is a
      statement about 37 galaxies in the mean, and the mean rather than the median gives -15% because four
      near-unresolved dwarfs pull it.  Both are printed above.
  (4) The ladder-free H_0 is 68.7 +- 3.4 (stat) on the canonical footing and 73.9 +- 3.6 on the alt -- landing on
      Planck and on SH0ES respectively.  The item's +-3 km/s/Mpc bar is met statistically and missed on systematics:
      Upsilon = 0.5 +- 0.1 alone is +-5.9, the footing is +5.2, and the flow-versus-TRGB zero point is
      7.4 km/s/Mpc -- a quadrature floor of 9.5, which is 2.8x the statistical error.  It cannot arbitrate the
      Hubble tension, and saying so is the result.
  (4b) What CAN be quoted is a cluster distance.  The 25 Ursa Major members give D = 17.7 +- 0.9 Mpc with a
      per-galaxy scatter of 0.100 dex (26%) -- the estimator's own precision, measured on a sample at one
      distance, and an upper bound on it because the cluster's real depth is inside that number.  The zero point is
      the same RAR normalisation as (3), so it is one measurement, not two; the scatter is the new part.
  (5) The one Upsilon-free handle -- twelve gas-dominated TRGB galaxies -- pulls a_0 to ~7e-11, BELOW canonical.
      Recorded against interest; twelve galaxies is not a measurement.
  (6) This supersedes the repo's earlier ladder-free H_0 lane (prep_2026/ladder_free_h0/, the E4 pair estimator,
      which returned 58-240 km/s/Mpc): same claim of ladder-freedom, same Upsilon nuisance, but the band is now
      a factor 20 narrower because the RAR itself, rather than a pair-difference, does the work.

